Q: What is a Transaction in MySQL?

A transaction is a sequence of SQL operations treated as **one unit of work**.
Either **all** succeed (COMMIT) or **none** stay applied (ROLLBACK) → protects data integrity.

Remember ACID:
- Atomicity  — all or nothing
- Consistency — valid state → valid state
- Isolation  — concurrent transactions don’t illegally interfere
- Durability — after commit, data survives crash (engine-dependent; InnoDB yes)

────────────────────────────────────────
MySQL basic syntax (correct)

START TRANSACTION;   -- or: BEGIN;

UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance - 100 WHERE id = 1;
UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance + 100 WHERE id = 2;

COMMIT;     -- save all changes
-- or
ROLLBACK;   -- undo all changes in this transaction

────────────────────────────────────────
Autocommit (important)

By default MySQL often has **autocommit=1**:
each statement is its own tiny transaction (auto COMMIT).

When you run START TRANSACTION / BEGIN:
- autocommit is effectively off until COMMIT or ROLLBACK
- You **do** need COMMIT to persist the group of statements
- Comment like “if autocommit enabled don’t need commit” is misleading for multi-statement work

Check/set:
SHOW VARIABLES LIKE 'autocommit';
SET autocommit = 0;   -- session

────────────────────────────────────────
Simple money-transfer example

START TRANSACTION;

UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance - 100 WHERE id = 1 AND balance >= 100;
-- optional: check ROW_COUNT() in app/procedure

UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance + 100 WHERE id = 2;

COMMIT;
-- on any failure in app code → ROLLBACK;

────────────────────────────────────────
⚠️ About the old sample

This is **not** valid MySQL style:
IF @@ERROR = 0 THEN ...  -- that’s SQL Server style

MySQL error handling in stored programs uses DECLARE HANDLER, etc.
In apps (Java/Spring), you catch exceptions and rollback the transaction.

────────────────────────────────────────
Spring Boot side (interview link)

@Transactional
public void transfer(Long from, Long to, BigDecimal amount) {
    // both updates in one transaction
}

- Success → commit
- RuntimeException → rollback (default)
- Works best on public methods of Spring beans (proxy)

────────────────────────────────────────
Engine note
Use **InnoDB** for real transactions.
MEMORY/MyISAM are not the transactional choice for durable multi-statement ACID work.

────────────────────────────────────────
Interview one-liner
“A transaction groups SQL into one atomic unit — COMMIT keeps all changes, ROLLBACK undoes them — giving ACID guarantees (with InnoDB).”
